useListenable is easy to use, but I think it tends to cause unnecessary rebuilds
In such a case, I think the useListenableSelector added this time is convenient.

This is an example where the button is disabled when nothing is entered in TextField.

Example using useListenable.
```dart
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    var ctrl = useTextEditingController();
    useListenable(ctrl);
    return Column(
      children: [
        TextField(
          controller: ctrl,
        ),
        ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: ctrl.text.isNotEmpty ? () => print("Pressed!") : null,
            child: Text("Button")),
      ],
    );
  }
```

Example using useListenableSelector.
This is the better performance.

```dart
 Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    final ctrl = useTextEditingController();
    final bool isNotEmpty =
        useListenableSelector(ctrl, () => ctrl.text.isNotEmpty);
    return Column(
      children: [
        TextField(controller: ctrl),
        ElevatedButton(
            // If no text is entered, the button cannot be pressed
            onPressed: isNotEmpty ? () => print("Pressed!") : null,
            child: Text("Button")),
      ],
    );
  }
```

This only worked in some cases, like non-ordinal encoded, now it
works consistently for all cases.

